  { state: "Connecticut",  adultsUse: "Yes (Jan 2023)", taxRate: "10% retail + 3% gross receipts", estPrice: "n/a", notes: "Mature medical, growing adult-use" },
  { state: "Colorado",     adultsUse: "Yes (Dec 2012)", taxRate: "15% retail + 15% excise", estPrice: "$5–$8/g typical", notes: "Mature market, declining wholesale" },
  { state: "California",   adultsUse: "Yes (Jan 2018)", taxRate: "15% excise + cultivation tax", estPrice: "~$8–$10/g typical", notes: "Largest US market; tax structure under 2025 reform" },
  { state: "Illinois",     adultsUse: "Yes (Jan 2020)", taxRate: "10% retail + 7% excise", estPrice: "$15.80/g (most expensive, per cannabispromotions 2025)", notes: "High-tax, low-supply state" },
  { state: "Michigan",     adultsUse: "Yes (Dec 2019)", taxRate: "10% retail + 10% excise", estPrice: "$3.80/g (cheapest, per cannabispromotions 2025)", notes: "High-supply, low-price state" }
];

const faqItems = [
  { question: "How big is Maine's adult-use cannabis market right now?", answer: `Maine's adult-use (recreational) cannabis market generated $246.4 million in retail sales across 4,835,678 individual transactions during calendar year 2025 — a 1.0% increase over the $243.9M sold in 2024 (Office of Cannabis Policy, Adult Use Cannabis Program 2025 Annual Report). As of Dec 31, 2025 the OCP counted ${siteStats.activeAdultUseRetailStores} active licensed cannabis retail stores and ${siteStats.activeAdultUseMunicipalities} host municipalities. The live OCP licensee-search roster shows ${liveOcp.auRetailStores} active retail stores in ${liveOcp.auMunicipalities} municipalities as of ${liveOcp.asOf}, reflecting normal license turnover. The market's first half of 2026 (Jan–Jun) totaled $119.95M in retail sales across 2,439,812 transactions at a year-to-date average flower price of $6.10/gram, putting Maine on pace for $244–248M in 2026 — a "plateau" year described by MaineBiz (April 20, 2026) as "the smallest year-over-year increase in the market so far."` },
  { question: "Has Maine's cannabis market kept growing, or is it plateauing?", answer: `Adult-use sales plateaued in 2025 (+1.0% YoY) after three consecutive years of double-digit growth: 2022 (+93.8%), 2023 (+36.5%), 2024 (+12.4%), 2025 (+1.0%). The plateau shows up across three OCP-reported metrics simultaneously: total dollars flat, total transactions up modestly (+4.7%), and average flower price declining (−8.6%, from $7.25 to $6.62 per gram). Together those metrics indicate the market is now a redistribution story — total consumer spend flat, but pricing is being competed away to consumers while operators absorb the margin compression. The market-pulse interpretation: Maine has moved from "growth at all costs" (2021–2023) through "rationalization" (2024) and into "maturation/consolidation" (2025–2026). Industry analysts at MJBizDaily and Cannabismarketcap describe the 2026 national picture similarly — hundreds of operators exiting, stronger MSOs absorbing market share — but Maine-specific dynamics (tourism, the caregiver program, and the Jan 1 2026 tax-restructuring) shape the local outcome.` },
  { question: "How much tax revenue does Maine collect from cannabis?", answer: `Maine collected approximately $43.72 million in Adult Use Cannabis Program (AUCP) registration-and-fee revenue during calendar year 2025 (per the OCP 2025 MMCP and AUCP annual reports via mainecannabis.org; revenue category = AUCP total revenue). On top of that, an estimated $14.28 million in sales tax revenue was deposited into the General Fund from medical cannabis in fiscal year 2025 (July 1, 2024 – June 30, 2025), reflecting the 5.5% state sales tax on cannabis sales (8% on edible cannabis products as prepared food). Cumulative AUCP revenue since adult-use sales began in 2020 totals roughly $163 million through FY2025. The January 1, 2026 biennial budget restructure (P.L. 2025, ch. 388) cut cultivation excise taxes by 30–43% but raised the retail sales tax on adult-use cannabis from 10.0% to 14.0% — a net ~$5.2M annual revenue uplift to the General Fund per the Dept. of Administrative and Financial Services fiscal note.` },
  { question: "What is the average price per gram of cannabis flower in Maine?", answer: `The OCP-reported average retail price of adult-use cannabis flower was $6.62 per gram in calendar year 2025, down from $7.25 in 2024 (an 8.6% decline). On a monthly basis, December 2025 alone averaged $6.30/gram — the lowest full-month price OCP has reported — with the first half of 2026 (Jan–Jun) running at a $6.10 year-to-date average. The lower bound is set by wholesale cultivator margins and the $223/lb flower excise tax effective Jan 1, 2026 (down from the prior $335/lb). <strong>How Maine compares nationally</strong>: the cleanest direct national comparable is Headset's June 2026 retail-POS tracker (12-market panel), which reports $8.94 per gram for single-gram packages and $3.62 per gram equivalized across all package sizes. The previously-cited $8.92/gram national average traces to Cannabis Benchmarks 2025 via Cannabis Promotions (an aggregator); Headset's $8.94 is the same data point with a one-step-fresher primary trail. Maine flower is therefore 26% below the national single-gram average (at $6.62 vs $8.94), and 22% above the national equivalized average (at $6.62 vs $3.62) — a pattern that reflects Maine's heavier weighting toward bulk-gram sales (where wholesale-to-retail margins are tighter). The cheapest state in the cannabispromotions 2025 table is Michigan at $3.80/gram; the most expensive is Illinois at $15.80/gram. Premium indoor-grown flower typically retails 30–50% above the average, putting top-shelf eighths at $30–$45 before tax.` },
  { question: "Where is Maine cannabis revenue concentrated geographically?", answer: `Maine's adult-use sales are highly concentrated in southern Maine. Per OCP Analytics Director Eric Miller's Oct 10, 2025 5th-anniversary briefing to the Joint Standing Committee on Veterans and Legal Affairs (reported by Maine Public, Portland Press Herald, and Sun Journal), York + Cumberland counties combined account for "nearly 60%" of all Maine adult-use cannabis retail sales. York County carries roughly 31% of state sales (~$76M in 2025) and Cumberland County roughly 28% (~$69M) — about 59% in just two of Maine's 16 counties. The remaining ~41% of state sales is distributed across the other 14 counties, with Penobscot (Bangor metro, ~9%), Androscoggin (Lewiston + Auburn, ~7%), and Kennebec (Augusta + Waterville, ~5%) rounding out the top five. This concentration reflects both population density and the licensed-retailer footprint — those two counties host 95 of the state's ${siteStats.activeAdultUseRetailStores} active retail stores — but it also captures cross-border demand from NH and MA residents who lack legal access and drive into Maine for adult-use purchases.` },
  { question: "How many municipalities in Maine have opted in to adult-use cannabis retail?", answer: `As of December 31, 2025, the OCP Adult Use Cannabis Program 2025 Annual Report counts ${siteStats.activeAdultUseMunicipalities} municipalities hosting active adult-use cannabis retail establishments. The live OCP licensee-search roster identifies active stores in ${liveOcp.auMunicipalities} municipalities as of ${liveOcp.asOf} — the gap reflects retail locations that have an active OCP license but have not yet reported their first sale, are inactive, or are reported under a mailing address that differs from their host municipality. Of Maine's 488 municipalities, roughly 65 (13.3%) have at least one active retail license, but only about 30 have active retail storefronts open to the public. The rest have authorized retail under local ordinance but not yet attracted a licensee. The MDG 2026 Opt-In Tracker publishes the full list of opted-in vs. opted-out towns; visitors should cross-check current status with the town clerk before relying on a specific number.` },
  { question: "Is Maine's cannabis market growing or shrinking in 2026?", answer: `Both, depending on the metric. By dollars: flat-to-slightly-up. By transactions: up modestly. By operators: down (consolidation). Through H1 2026, Maine posted $119,954,243 in adult-use retail sales across 2,439,812 transactions — an annualized run-rate of $244–248M, consistent with the 2025 plateau (MJBizDaily + Marijuana Herald, July 2026). Simultaneously, the number of active retail stores on the OCP roster has tightened from 187 (Dec 31, 2025 Annual Report peak) to ${liveOcp.auRetailStores} active licensee-search roster entries as of ${liveOcp.asOf} — the gap is normal license turnover (closures, conditional approvals still pending), not a crash. The 2026 outlook factors in: (1) the January 1, 2026 tax restructure raising the retail sales tax from 10% to 14% — which lifts consumer shelf prices ~3.6% unless operators absorb it; (2) the P.L. 2025, ch. 764 emergency testing-reform law effective April 19, 2026 that eliminates mandatory contaminant testing for edibles made from already-tested concentrate, reducing manufacturing friction; and (3) continuous downward price pressure on flower as the supply side reaches steady state. Net read: dollars flat, transactions slightly up, active operator count slightly down — classic maturation.` },
  { question: "How does the Maine cannabis market compare to the US as a whole?", answer: `Maine's $246.4 million adult-use market is a small fraction of the US total — Statista (2026, cited via Flowhub) projects the US cannabis market at $47 billion in 2026 and $55.4 billion by 2030 at a 3.36% CAGR. Maine's 2024 actual (and 2025 actual as reported by OCP) represents roughly 0.5% of total US legal cannabis spend — comparable to its 0.4% share of US population, meaning Maine is a per-capita-consistent market, not an over- or under-performer. On a per-resident basis, Maine's $176/capita adult-use spend (CY2025) sits close to the national median estimated by BDSA per-state breakdowns. The US market itself is bifurcating: mature states (Colorado, California, Oregon) are showing flat or declining revenue per the December 2025 Whitney Economics downward revision ($29.1–29.6B 2025 forecast vs $30.1B 2024 actual), while newer legal states (New York, New Mexico, Missouri) are still in ramp. Maine's 2025 plateau mirrors the mature-state pattern, which is the right read of the data — the national story is "growth by geography, plateau by maturity."` },
  { question: "How many Americans use cannabis, and what does that say about demand in Maine?", answer: `The 2024 National Survey on Drug Use and Health (SAMHSA NSDUH 2024, HHS Pub PEP25-07-007, released August 2025) is the cleanest federal demand snapshot. Among Americans aged 12 or older, 15.4% (44.3 million people) used cannabis in the past month, and 22.3% (64.2 million) used in the past year. Both metrics are up meaningfully from 2021 (13.2% / 37.0M past-month). The 2024 release is the first NSDUH to break out adult-use cannabis use separately from total cannabis use under the new "Marijuana Use and Marijuana Vaping in the Past Month" header. Maine's OCP data — 4,835,678 adult-use transactions in 2025 across 1.4M residents — implies a per-capita transaction count of 3.45 per resident per year, which is consistent with the SAMHSA past-month rate applied to Maine's adult population (~1.1M adults). The implied takeaway: Maine's per-capita adult-use participation is in line with national rates, with the residual above-state-typical (after subtracting demographic and age adjustment) attributable to cross-border demand from New Hampshire and Massachusetts. Important methodology note: 2021–2024 NSDUH data is NOT directly comparable to 2020 and earlier because SAMHSA switched to multimode data collection in 2021; trend claims must respect that break.` },
  { question: "What is the current federal legal status of cannabis, and how does it affect Maine operators?", answer: `As of July 9, 2026, federal cannabis regulation is in a three-tier transitional state: (1) <strong>FDA-approved marijuana products</strong> (e.g., Marinol) and <strong>state-licensed medical cannabis</strong> — including Maine's Medical Use of Cannabis Program — are <strong>Schedule III</strong> as of April 22, 2026, per DEA final rule (91 FR doc 2026-08176); (2) <strong>adult-use (recreational) cannabis</strong> in all 24 legal states — including Maine's Adult Use Cannabis Program — remains <strong>Schedule I</strong> at the federal level under 21 U.S.C. §812 and 21 CFR §1308.11; (3) the <strong>broader Schedule I → III rescheduling NPRM</strong> that would reclassify all marijuana (including adult-use) is pending a DEA Administrative Law Judge hearing that ran June 29 – July 15, 2026. The Schedule III status for state medical programs is a meaningful change: cannabis businesses can now take ordinary business deductions (the 280E barrier that drove ~$15B in excess federal tax since 2018 per Whitney Economics), at least for medical revenue. Adult-use revenue remains 280E-blocked. For Maine operators, the practical implications are: medical-side operators gain a federal tax deduction; adult-use operators wait on the ALJ outcome; and 280E remains the single largest non-tax expense for adult-use operators nationally.` }
];

