    <p class="stat-source">
      Primary sources: OCP Adult Use Cannabis Program 2025 Annual Report (28-B M.R.S. §113); OCP Medical Use of Cannabis Program 2025 Annual Report (22 M.R.S. §2430-N); mainecannabis.org monthly Maine Revenue Services sales reports; Marijuana Herald reporting from OCP data releases (Jan &amp; Jul 2026).
    </p>

    <Callout title="Per-capita context" type="info">
      $246.4M divided by ~1.4M residents yields an adult-use per-capita spend of about <strong>$176 per resident per year</strong> — well above the national median and a function of the unusually heavy cross-border demand from New Hampshire, Massachusetts, and Quebec. Add in medical cannabis ($252.8M in MRS-reported sales, 2025) and total legal cannabis spend is closer to <strong>$499M</strong> statewide — roughly $357 per resident across all ages.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="multi-year">
    <h2>Adult-Use Sales, 2020 – H1 2026 — A Five-Year Arc</h2>

    <p>Adult-use (recreational) cannabis sales launched in Maine on October 9, 2020. The first five full calendar years of the program show three distinct phases: <strong>launch ramp</strong> (2021), <strong>double-digit growth</strong> (2022–2024), and <strong>maturation/plateau</strong> (2025). The 2026 half-year data continues the plateau.</p>

    <figure class="page-video">
      <video
        src="/videos/market-stats.mp4"
        poster="/videos/market-stats.poster.jpg"
        muted
        playsinline
        loop
        preload="metadata"
        controls
      ></video>
      <figcaption>35-second summary of the 2025 OCP data — sales, transactions, stores, tax revenue, and the H1 2026 forecast. Press play when you want to watch; use the speaker control to unmute.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <table class="year-table" aria-label="Maine adult-use cannabis retail sales 2020 to first half 2026">
      <caption>Table 1 — Maine adult-use cannabis retail sales, 2020–H1 2026. Adult-use retail launched October 9, 2020.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Period</th>
          <th class="num-col">Retail Sales ($M)</th>
          <th class="num-col">YoY Change</th>
          <th class="num-col">Transactions</th>
          <th class="num-col">Avg Flower Price</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {auSalesHistory.map((r, i) => (
          <tr class={i === 5 ? 'highlight' : ''}>
            <td><strong>{r.year}</strong></td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.sales?.toFixed(1) ?? '—'}{r.sales != null ? 'M' : ''}</td>
            <td class="num-col">
              {r.year === '2025' ? '+1.0%' :
               r.year === 'H1 2026' ? 'n/a (YTD)' :
               r.year === '2020*' ? '—' :
               r.year === '2021' ? 'first full year' :
               r.year === '2022' ? '+93.8%' :
               r.year === '2023' ? '+36.5%' :
               r.year === '2024' ? '+12.4%' : ''}
            </td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.transactions ?? '—'}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.avgPrice ?? '—'}</td>
            <td>{r.notes}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="stat-source">
      Sources: $246.4M / 4,835,678 transactions / $6.62 avg price for CY2025 — Marijuana Herald (Jan 2026) reporting OCP data release. H1 2026 $119,954,243 / 2,439,812 transactions / $6.10 avg price — Marijuana Herald (July 2026). 2020–2024 figures — mainecannabis.org monthly aggregation of Maine Revenue Services adult-use-cannabis taxable sales, reconciled to OCP annual reports. Note: 2025 figure is OCP-released; the 2020–2024 series is reported as MRS-adult-use-sales (a narrower tax-base than the OCP total but consistent under the same reporting regime).
    </p>

    <h3>What the five-year curve tells us</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The launch ramp (2021) added $77.6M</strong> in year-one sales — a 19x jump off the $4.35M partial-year 2020 base. This is the period when supply, consumer awareness, and store count were all ramping together.</li>
      <li><strong>Growth years (2022–2024) added $161.9M</strong> in cumulative incremental sales — roughly $54M of growth per year, driven by new store openings and price normalization.</li>
      <li><strong>The plateau (2025) added only $2.5M</strong> over 2024. But: average price fell $0.63/gram (8.6%), implying that the <em>physical units sold</em> grew faster than dollar revenue. Per the Marijuana Herald December 2025 readout, unit volume in December 2025 alone was 388,831 transactions — consistent with steady volume growth on a price-deflated basis.</li>
      <li><strong>H1 2026 numbers</strong> ($119.95M, 2.44M transactions, $6.10/g YTD) are <em>consistent with</em> an annualized total of $244–248M for full-year 2026 — i.e., another plateau year. There is no evidence in the H1 data of a Q2 acceleration that would project back to a 2026 total above $250M.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="establishments">
    <h2>Active Licensed Establishments by Type</h2>

    <p>Adult-use cannabis licensing in Maine occurs at four tiers under 28-B M.R.S. §207. Total active adult-use establishment counts crossed 300 in 2023 and have been growing at low single-digit rates since.</p>

    <table class="year-table" aria-label="Maine adult-use cannabis licensed establishment counts by type">
      <caption>Table 2 — Active adult-use cannabis establishments, Dec 31, 2025. Source: OCP 2025 Annual Report.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>License Type</th>
          <th class="num-col">Active Count</th>
          <th>Net Change (2024 → 2025)</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {estabTypes.map(r => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.type}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.count}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.growth}</td>
            <td>{r.notes ?? ''}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="stat-source">
      Source: OCP Adult Use Cannabis Program 2024 + 2025 Annual Reports (28-B M.R.S. §113). Note the difference between <em>active</em> (currently licensed) and <em>operating</em> (currently open and selling) — Maine's licensed-but-not-yet-operating gap is usually 5–15% of the active total. The cross-program contrast is sharp: adult-use retailer counts are flat, while medical dispensary counts (<a href="#medical-program">see below</a>) grew 28.5% in 2025.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="medical-program">
    <h2>Medical Program, 2021 – 2025</h2>

    <p>The Maine Medical Use of Cannabis Program (MMCP) is the older of the two regulated cannabis markets and behaves counter-cyclically to adult-use. As adult-use plateaued in 2025, medical patient certifications, medical providers, and registered dispensaries all grew — while the caregiver population continued its multi-year decline.</p>

    <table class="year-table" aria-label="Maine medical cannabis program metrics 2021 to 2025">
      <caption>Table 3 — MMCP key indicators, 2021–2025. Source: OCP MMCP Annual Reports to the Legislature (22 M.R.S. §2430-N).</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Year</th>
          <th class="num-col">Patient Certifications</th>
          <th class="num-col">Medical Providers</th>
          <th class="num-col">Registered Caregivers</th>
          <th class="num-col">Registered Dispensaries</th>
          <th class="num-col">Employees (RICs)</th>
          <th>Program Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {medicalHistory.map(r => (
          <tr class={r.year === 2025 ? 'highlight' : ''}>
            <td><strong>{r.year}</strong></td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.certifications.toLocaleString()}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.providers}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.caregivers.toLocaleString()}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.dispensaries ?? '—'}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.employees ? r.employees.toLocaleString() : '—'}</td>
            <td>{r.notes}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="stat-source">
      Source: OCP Medical Use of Cannabis Program 2025 Annual Report (Feb 13, 2026) reporting on 22 M.R.S. §2430-N requirements. Patient-certification counts include reprints (per OCP methodology); "registrant-related employment" series starts at 4,358 in 2024 because the registry identification card (RIC) two-year-cycle reform took effect July 1, 2024.
    </p>

    <h3>What the medical numbers show</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dispensaries grew fastest.</strong> Registered medical dispensaries grew from 35 (2022) → 60 (2023) → 70 (2024) → 90 (2025), a 28.5% YoY expansion in 2025 alone — and 46 → 62 retail locations, +34.7% YoY. The 2025 expansion was fueled by caregivers who transitioned to formal dispensary status after the January 2021 dispensary-cap expiration and the PL 2023 c. 365 framework.</li>
      <li><strong>Patient volume is steady, not surging.</strong> Certifications grew 1.8% YoY in 2025 — slower than 2024 (+1.4%) but still positive. Compare this to the mature, flat-to-declining patient-count patterns in mature markets like Colorado or Oregon.</li>
      <li><strong>The caregiver population is shrinking.</strong> 1,539 active caregivers at year-end 2025 vs 1,677 in 2024 (−8.2%) — the seventh consecutive year of decline since the 2021 peak of 3,257. The caregiver exodus reflects PL 2023 c. 365 + PL 2023 c. 679 framework tightening, plus caregiver-to-dispensary transitions.</li>
      <li><strong>Provider count continues to climb.</strong> 808 active certifying providers in 2025 vs 777 in 2024 — a 3.9% increase, smaller than prior years. Providers remain concentrated: the top 10 (out of 808) issued 83,636 of 112,547 certifications (74.3% of all printed certifications statewide in 2025).</li>
    </ul>

    <Callout title="Caregiver context" type="note">
      286 registered caregivers reported operating a caregiver retail store in 2025 (MMCP 2025 Annual Report §4.2). Combined with the 62 registered medical-dispensary retail locations, Maine now has <strong>348 storefront-style medical retail points of sale</strong> — vs 187 adult-use retail stores. This makes the regulated medical side structurally larger than the adult-use side by storefront count, even though adult-use gross revenue ($246.4M) clearly exceeds the medical side's retail share.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="regional">
    <h2>Regional &amp; County Breakdown</h2>

    <p>Maine's adult-use cannabis sales are highly geographically concentrated. The OCP's Analytics Director, Eric Miller, told the Joint Standing Committee on Veterans and Legal Affairs at the 5th-anniversary briefing on October 10, 2025 that <em>"York and Cumberland counties combined account for nearly 60 percent of all retail sales."</em> (Maine Public; Portland Press Herald; Sun Journal.) This concentration tracks population density, tourism traffic, and the licensed-retailer footprint, but also reflects cross-border demand from New Hampshire and Massachusetts residents who can drive into Maine for legal adult-use cannabis purchases.</p>

    <table class="year-table" aria-label="Maine cannabis sales share by county">
      <caption>Table 4 — Estimated CY2025 adult-use sales share by county (totals 100%; rounded to nearest 1%).</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>County</th>
