    </table>

    <p class="stat-source">
      Sources: NORML press release (Sept 25, 2024) analyzing FBI Crime Data Explorer data. The 217,150 figure for 2023 represents a floor, not a ceiling: in 2022, 17% of agencies (representing 25% of the US population) failed to report to the FBI. The 2007 peak (870,000+ arrests, ~48% of all drug arrests) and the 2021 FBI methodology change mean pre-2021 vs post-2021 data is not directly comparable. The trend across 2007–2023 (870K → 217K, −75%) is the cleanest read of the post-legalization effect: a meaningful national decline in marijuana-related arrests, even as the drug has been legalized in 24 states.
    </p>

    <h3>Where this leaves Maine in the national picture</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Market position.</strong> Maine is a mature, plateau-state by national 2026 standards — adult-use growth has flattened at ~1% YoY while mature states (Colorado, California, Oregon) are now showing YoY declines. Maine's 2026 projected $244–248M is within 1% of 2025, consistent with the national "growth by geography, plateau by maturity" pattern documented by Whitney Economics (December 2025).</li>
      <li><strong>Price position.</strong> Maine flower at $6.62/g (OCP-weighted) is 26% below the $8.94 single-gram national average (Headset June 2026) — cheap for an East Coast state, comparable to West Coast mature markets, more expensive than the $3.62 equivalized national average (which weights toward bulk-gram and ounce packages).</li>
      <li><strong>Federal exposure.</strong> Maine's 24 adult-use stores + 90 medical dispensaries are split between Schedule III (medical) and Schedule I (adult-use) at the federal level as of July 9, 2026. The DEA ALJ hearing outcome (June 29 – July 15, 2026) will determine whether adult-use also gets Schedule III treatment; Maine operators should plan for both outcomes and consider medical/adult-use P&amp;L segregation as a defensive practice.</li>
      <li><strong>Operator economics.</strong> National cannabis-supported FTE jobs declined 3.4% YoY in 2025 (Vangst), the first YoY employment decline since legal markets began — a parallel signal to the 2025 revenue decline. Maine's own data shows a similar pattern: adult-use store count flat at 187, OCP roster tightening. The labor-market signal confirms the maturation read.</li>
      <li><strong>For consumer guides:</strong> if you're a shopper, the <a href="/guides/cannabis-terpenes-effects-maine">cannabis terpenes &amp; effects guide</a> covers what to look for; the <a href="/guides/cannabis-tinctures-sublingual-maine">tinctures guide</a> covers lower-risk entry formats; the <a href="/guides/cannabis-edible-dose-calculator-maine">edible dose calculator</a> helps with the most common 280E-irrelevant consumer math problem (over-consumption).</li>
      <li><strong>For operators:</strong> if you're modeling a new license, the <a href="/roi-calculator">ROI calculator</a> uses national and Maine benchmarks to project per-store economics; the <a href="/maine-cannabis-tax-calculator">tax calculator</a> handles the Jan 1, 2026 tax reset; the <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-market">Maine market analysis guide</a> gives the operator-facing context; the <a href="/guides/maine-dispensary-license">license requirements guide</a> walks the application step-by-step.</li>
      <li><strong>For municipal / investor readers:</strong> the <a href="/market-pulse-2026">market pulse 2026</a> page tracks quarterly license activity, opt-in coverage, and M&amp;A events — the companion dataset to this page for people who want the operator-footprint story.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

